{% extends "base.html.j2" %}
{# Pagina "Sources & method": fonti, controllo di freschezza, riserve, serie calcolate, limiti noti dei dati. #}

{% block titolo %}Sources &amp; method · Macro dashboard{% endblock %}
{% block descrizione %}Where the data comes from, how freshness is checked, what happens when a source fails, and the known limits of the data.{% endblock %}

{% block contenuto %}
<section class="intestazione-pagina">
  <h1>Sources &amp; method</h1>
  <p class="descrizione">Where every number comes from, how the site protects itself from stale or missing data, and what the
    data cannot tell you.</p>
  <p class="aggiornamento">Updated <strong>{{ aggiornato }}</strong></p>
  <ul class="chips" aria-label="On this page">
    <li><a href="#how-it-works">How it works</a></li>
    <li><a href="#sources">Sources</a></li>
    <li><a href="#freshness">Freshness check</a></li>
    <li><a href="#annual-data">Annual data</a></li>
    <li><a href="#activity">Build activity</a></li>
    <li><a href="#fallback">Fallback sources</a></li>
    <li><a href="#calculated">Calculated series</a></li>
    <li><a href="#weekly-moves">What changed this week</a></li>
    <li><a href="#limits">Known limits</a></li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="blocco" id="how-it-works">
  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <p class="descrizione">This is a static site. A script downloads every series, calculates what needs calculating and writes the pages;
    GitHub rebuilds and publishes it automatically on Monday to Saturday at 23:30 UTC, after the US futures markets and the Asian
    exchanges have closed. A single series that fails never blocks the site: it becomes "unavailable" and a warning appears
    at the top of the pages that use it. Prices are closing prices without dividends; rates are in % and changes in rates
    are shown in basis points (bp, 0.01%), changes in year-on-year inflation in percentage points (pp). This dashboard is
    for study purposes and is not financial advice.</p>
</section>

<section class="blocco" id="sources">
  <h2>Sources</h2>
  <p class="descrizione">The number of series is counted by main source. Every chart names its sources under the chart, with a link to the
    series on the source's website; the full list is in <a href="{{ radice }}method/series/">Series status</a>.</p>
  <div class="tabella-scorrevole">
    <table class="tabella-testo">
      <thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>What we use it for</th><th>Access</th><th class="num">Series</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {% for f in m.fonti %}
        <tr>
          <td>{% if f.url %}<a href="{{ f.url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">{{ f.nome }} ↗</a>{% else %}{{ f.nome }}{% endif %}</td>
          <td>{{ f.uso }}</td><td>{{ f.accesso }}</td><td class="num">{{ f.n_serie }}</td>
        </tr>
        {% endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="descrizione">Recession dates: NBER (series USREC on FRED) for the US; the CEPR for the euro area, copied by hand into the configuration
    because it has no API.</p>
</section>

<section class="blocco" id="freshness">
  <h2>Freshness check</h2>
  <p class="descrizione">Every series is checked after each download. If its latest data point is older than the threshold for its frequency,
    a warning appears at the top of the pages that use it. It catches "frozen" series: the source answers without any error but has
    stopped adding data (for example when a dataset is retired and replaced by another). Days are counted from the <em>end</em> of the period of
    the latest data point, so the August value of a monthly series counts as fresh until 31 August.</p>
  <div class="tabella-scorrevole">
    <table class="tabella-testo tabella-stretta">
      <thead><tr><th>Frequency</th><th class="num">Threshold (days)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{% for s in m.soglie %}<tr><td>{{ s.frequenza }}</td><td class="num">{{ s.giorni }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  {% if m.soglie_proprie %}
  <p class="descrizione">A few series have their own threshold, always for a documented reason:
    {% for s in m.soglie_proprie %}<strong>{{ s.nome }}</strong> ({{ s.giorni }} days){{ ", " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}
    — the BIS publishes South Korea with about a month of delay (see <a href="#note-korea-bis-delay">Korea policy rate delay</a>).</p>
  {% endif %}
  {% if in_ritardo %}
  <p class="descrizione"><strong>Right now {{ in_ritardo|length }} {{ 'series is' if in_ritardo|length == 1 else 'series are' }} stale:</strong>
    {% for s in in_ritardo %}{{ s.nome }} (latest data {{ s.data }}){{ ", " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}.</p>
  {% else %}
  <p class="descrizione"><strong>Right now no series is stale.</strong></p>
  {% endif %}
</section>

<section class="blocco" id="annual-data">
  <h2>Annual data by country</h2>
  <p class="descrizione">Country data that changes only once or twice a year (GDP, inflation, government balance and debt and the IMF projections from the
    World Economic Outlook, WEO; unemployment, population and governance indicators from the World Bank) is not downloaded at every build.
    It sits in files in the repository (a "snapshot"): the WEO is refreshed by hand after each release, in April and October (the October 2026 edition is
    due in mid-October), and the World Bank files after its releases (July for the development indicators, September for the governance indicators).
    The daily build only reads the files. In the IMF snapshot every figure after the country's <em>latest actual year</em> is an estimate or a projection,
    and the site will draw it differently. The freshness check counts from the publication date: a warning appears if the IMF snapshot is more than
    210 days old (six months plus about four weeks, so a new edition does not trigger a false alarm) or a World Bank snapshot more than 456 days
    (fifteen months). Details of each indicator are in <a href="{{ radice }}method/series/#annual-indicators">Series status</a>.</p>
  <div class="tabella-scorrevole">
    <table class="tabella-testo tabella-stretta">
      <thead><tr><th>Snapshot</th><th>Edition / updated</th><th>Frequency</th><th class="num">Age (days)</th><th class="num">Threshold</th><th class="num">Countries</th><th class="num">Indicators</th><th>Now</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {% for a in m.annuali %}
        <tr>
          <td>{{ a.nome }}</td><td>{% if a.presente %}{{ a.edizione + ", published " if a.edizione }}{{ a.data }}{% else %}—{% endif %}</td><td>{{ a.frequenza }}</td>
          <td class="num">{{ a.giorni if a.presente else "—" }}</td><td class="num">{{ a.soglia }}</td><td class="num">{{ a.n_paesi }}</td><td class="num">{{ a.n_indicatori }}</td>
          <td>{% if not a.presente %}<span class="stato-errore">⚠ no snapshot</span>{% elif a.in_ritardo %}<span class="stato-ritardo">ⓘ stale</span>{% else %}<span class="stato-ok">✓ fresh</span>{% endif %}</td>
        </tr>
        {% endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blocco" id="activity">
  <h2>Build activity</h2>
  <p class="descrizione">The site is rebuilt every day by a scheduled GitHub workflow. GitHub switches off scheduled workflows of public repositories
    after 60 days without repository activity, silently; its documentation does not say what counts as activity (commits and pushes do, according to
    user reports; a push made by a workflow itself may not). To notice in time, every build reads the date of the latest commit and shows a warning
    on every page if more than {{ m.attivita.soglia if m.attivita else 45 }} days have passed.
    {% if m.attivita %}<strong>Latest commit: {{ m.attivita.ultimo }}, {{ m.attivita.giorni }} days ago.</strong>{% else %}<strong>The date of the latest commit could not be read in this build.</strong>{% endif %}</p>
</section>

<section class="blocco" id="fallback">
  <h2>Fallback sources</h2>
  <p class="descrizione">Some series have a second source, used only if the main one fails. When it happens the chart footer says
    "(fallback)" and a notice appears on the page. A fallback can be a slightly different quantity (for example a spot price instead of a
    future, or a monthly average instead of a daily value), a different unit, or a series with a shorter history: the difference is
    written in the notice and in the table below.
    {% if riserve %}<strong>Right now {{ riserve|length }} {{ 'series is' if riserve|length == 1 else 'series are' }} on a fallback.</strong>{% else %}<strong>Right now every series uses its main source.</strong>{% endif %}</p>
  <div class="tabella-scorrevole">
    <table class="tabella-testo">
      <thead><tr><th>Series</th><th>Main source</th><th>Fallback</th><th>Difference</th><th>Now</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {% for f in m.fallback %}
        <tr><td>{{ f.nome }}</td><td><code>{{ f.principale }}</code></td><td><code>{{ f.riserva }}</code></td><td>{{ f.differenze }}</td>
          <td>{% if f.in_uso %}<span class="stato-ritardo">ⓘ fallback in use</span>{% else %}<span class="stato-ok">✓ main</span>{% endif %}</td></tr>
        {% endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blocco" id="calculated">
  <h2>Calculated series</h2>
  <p class="descrizione">{{ m.regola_calcolate }}</p>
  <div class="tabella-scorrevole">
    <table class="tabella-testo">
      <thead><tr><th>Series</th><th>Calculation</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {% for c in m.calcolate %}
        <tr><td>{{ c.nome }}</td><td>{{ c.formula }}</td>
          <td>{% if c.ok %}<span class="stato-ok">✓ calculated</span>{% else %}<span class="stato-errore">⚠ unavailable: {{ c.errore }}</span>{% endif %}</td></tr>
        {% endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blocco" id="weekly-moves">
  <h2>What changed this week</h2>
  <p class="descrizione">The Overview lists the biggest weekly moves compared with each series' own normal volatility, so that a 20 bp move
    in a Treasury yield and a 5% move in oil can be compared. For each daily or weekly series flagged for this purpose, the site takes the
    change over the last {{ m.movimenti.giorni }} days and divides it by the typical size of {{ m.movimenti.giorni }}-day changes in the
    {{ m.movimenti.anni }} years before that week (the standard deviation, from at least {{ m.movimenti.minimo }} observations). The result is
    shown as "×normal": 3.0× means three times a normal week. Rates and spreads are measured in basis points, prices and indices in %.</p>
  <p class="descrizione">Only moves of at least <strong>{{ m.movimenti.soglia }}×</strong> are listed, at most one series per group (all US Treasury
    yields count as one, for example, because they usually move together) and at most {{ m.movimenti.massimo }} rows. If nothing reaches the bar,
    the box says "A quiet week".</p>
  <p class="descrizione"><strong>Why {{ m.movimenti.soglia }}× and not a lower bar?</strong> If weekly moves were normally distributed, about 13% of
    series would exceed 1.5× in an ordinary week purely by chance, so the list would almost always be full and would lose its meaning. At
    {{ m.movimenti.soglia }}× that share falls to about 5%, so the list fills up mainly when something unusual really happens, and "A quiet week"
    becomes useful information. Real markets have fatter tails and many series move together, so in a turbulent week the share can be much higher:
    on the latest build, {{ m.movimenti.n_sopra_soglia }} of {{ m.movimenti.n_controllate }} series exceeded {{ m.movimenti.soglia }}×.</p>
  <p class="descrizione"><strong>A score is not a probability.</strong> It says how many times larger than a typical week the move was, not how rare
    it is. The VIX, for example, moves a lot even in normal weeks, so it rarely reaches the bar. Policy rates are left out because they change in
    steps, monthly series because they have no weekly move, and any series with fewer than {{ m.movimenti.minimo }} past observations or an
    out-of-date last value.</p>
  <details class="stato">
    <summary>The {{ m.movimenti.n_serie }} series in the calculation, by group</summary>
    <div class="tabella-scorrevole">
      <table class="tabella-testo">
        <thead><tr><th>Group (one row at most)</th><th>Series</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{% for g in m.movimenti.gruppi %}<tr><td>{{ g.nome }}</td><td>{{ g.serie }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}</tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </details>
</section>

<section class="blocco" id="limits">
  <h2>Known limits</h2>
  <p class="descrizione">The technical notes that used to sit under the charts. Under each chart you will find links to the notes that apply to it.</p>
  {% for gruppo in m.gruppi_note %}
  <h3 class="titolo-gruppo">{{ gruppo.titolo }}</h3>
  <dl class="note-lista">
    {% for n in gruppo.note %}
    <div class="nota-voce" id="note-{{ n.id }}">
      <dt>{{ n.titolo }}</dt>
      <dd>{{ n.testo }}
        {% if n.usi %}<span class="usi">Used on:
          {% for u in n.usi %}<a href="{{ radice }}{{ u.href }}">{{ u.titolo }}</a>{{ ", " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}</span>{% endif %}
      </dd>
    </div>
    {% endfor %}
  </dl>
  {% endfor %}
</section>
{% endblock %}
